---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AiSummary from '../../components/AiSummary.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/Byline.astro';
import { site, teamBySlug } from '../../data/site';
import { outcomesSurvey } from '../../data/research';
import { stats } from '../../data/quality-index';
import bls from '../../data/bls.json';

const author = teamBySlug['donald-lewis'];
const rdn = bls.occupations['dietitians-nutritionists'];

const datasetSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: outcomesSurvey.name,
  description: outcomesSurvey.method,
  creator: { '@id': `${site.url}/#organization` },
  datePublished: outcomesSurvey.published,
  temporalCoverage: '2020/2025',
  variableMeasured: [
    'employment within six months of graduation',
    'median starting salary by credential',
    'first career path',
    'employer-requested skills',
    'importance of supervised practice'
  ],
  url: new URL('/resources/nutrition-career-outcomes/', site.url).toString()
};

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Is the 86 percent employment figure a national rate?',
    answer: `No. It is the share of ${outcomesSurvey.sample.toLocaleString()} self-selected respondents in the ${outcomesSurvey.name} who reported employment within six months of graduation. Graduates who left the field or lost touch with their programs are harder to reach, so treat 86 percent as a ceiling, not a census.`
  },
  {
    question: 'Are the salary figures federal data?',
    answer: `No. Median starting salaries by credential are ours: $52,000 certificate, $58,000 associate, $65,000 bachelor, $75,000 master, $85,000 doctorate. They are self-reported first-position base pay and are not adjusted for region. The Bureau of Labor Statistics May 2024 median of $${rdn.median.toLocaleString()} for dietitians and nutritionists is a full-career occupational median, not an entry figure. We never blend the two silently.`
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does supervised practice dominate the findings?',
    answer: `${outcomesSurvey.headline.supervisedPracticeCritical} percent of respondents called supervised practice critical or very important, the strongest consensus in the study. It is also an RDN eligibility requirement. Students who treat internship placement as a final-year problem are ignoring the finding that most clearly predicts whether this education becomes a career.`
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I cite this survey?',
    answer: `Yes. Cite it as "${outcomesSurvey.name}, NutritionSchools.org, ${outcomesSurvey.published}." Carry the limitations with any figure you quote. The dedicated research page and this page exist so those caveats stay attached.`
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Nutrition and Dietetics Career Outcomes Survey 2026"
  description={`Proprietary survey of ${outcomesSurvey.sampleLabel}: ${outcomesSurvey.headline.employedWithinSixMonths} percent employed within six months, starting salaries by credential, career paths, skills in demand, and the limitations you should read before citing.`}
  article={{ section: 'Research', author: author.name, published: outcomesSurvey.published }}
>
  <script slot="head" is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(datasetSchema)} />
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Original research</p>
      <h1>Nutrition and Dietetics Career Outcomes Survey 2026</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {outcomesSurvey.sampleLabel} from the {outcomesSurvey.cohort}, fielded {outcomesSurvey.fieldedLabel}. These are
        our numbers. The limitations sit next to the findings, not in a footnote.
      </p>
      <div class="metrics">
        <div><strong>{outcomesSurvey.sample.toLocaleString()}</strong><span>graduates surveyed</span></div>
        <div><strong>{outcomesSurvey.headline.employedWithinSixMonths}%</strong><span>employed within six months</span></div>
        <div><strong>{outcomesSurvey.headline.supervisedPracticeCritical}%</strong><span>called supervised practice critical</span></div>
        <div><strong>{outcomesSurvey.headline.employerHiringDifficulty}%</strong><span>of employers struggle to hire RDNs</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="content wrap">
    <Byline author={author} prefix="Research directed by" />

    <AiSummary>
      The Nutrition and Dietetics Career Outcomes Survey 2026 is proprietary research by NutritionSchools.org covering
      1,154 nutrition and dietetics graduates from the classes of 2020 through 2025, fielded January to March 2026.
      {outcomesSurvey.headline.employedWithinSixMonths} percent reported employment within six months.
      Median self-reported first-position salaries were $52,000 at certificate level, $58,000 associate, $65,000
      bachelor, $75,000 master, and $85,000 doctorate. Top first paths were clinical dietetics (28 percent), community
      and public health nutrition (20 percent), and food service management (14 percent). Medical nutrition therapy was
      the most requested skill at 72 percent. {outcomesSurvey.headline.supervisedPracticeCritical} percent called
      supervised practice critical or very important. {outcomesSurvey.headline.employerHiringDifficulty} percent of
      employers reported difficulty finding qualified RDN candidates, and
      {outcomesSurvey.headline.wantedBusinessTraining} percent of graduates wished they had more business training.
      Respondents opted in, so the employment figure is a ceiling. Last updated August 2026.
    </AiSummary>

    <div class="hot-take">
      <strong>The finding that should change how you enroll</strong>
      <p>
        The RDN credential is not optional if you want clinical work, and supervised practice is not a formality.
        {outcomesSurvey.headline.supervisedPracticeCritical} percent of graduates said it was critical or very important
        to their career. Students still try to skip it. That is a career-ending mistake, and this survey is why we say so
        in those words.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What this survey is, and what it is not</h2>
    <p>{outcomesSurvey.method}</p>
    <p>
      It is not a Bureau of Labor Statistics product. Federal wage and projection figures on this site come from the May
      2024 Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics release and the 2024 to 2034 Employment Projections program, cited
      by name on career pages. This survey answers a different question: what happened to a specific cohort of nutrition
      and dietetics graduates at first position, in their own words.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anyone quoting 86 percent employment without the self-selection caveat is misusing our data. We would rather you
      cite us accurately than cite us generously.
    </p>
    <p>
      The research record that also houses the Program Quality Index is on
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/research/">the research page</a>. Scoring rules for the rankings that cite this
      survey sit on <a class="text-link" href="/methodology/">methodology</a>. This page exists so the survey can be
      cited without mixing it into either of those.
    </p>

    <h2>Key findings, in the order they should change a decision</h2>
    <p>
      Three results dominate, and they are not the ones most program pages advertise. First,
      {' '}{outcomesSurvey.headline.supervisedPracticeCritical} percent of graduates said supervised practice was
      critical or very important to their career. That is the strongest consensus in the study, stronger than any
      opinion about coursework, prestige, or cost. Second, {outcomesSurvey.headline.employerHiringDifficulty} percent of
      employers reported difficulty finding qualified RDN candidates. Third,
      {' '}{outcomesSurvey.headline.wantedBusinessTraining} percent of graduates wished they had more business training
      for private practice. Nutrition curricula do not allocate time in that order, which is why graduates notice the
      gap after they leave.
    </p>
    <p>
      Employment within six months was {outcomesSurvey.headline.employedWithinSixMonths} percent. That is a real number
      from a real sample, and it is also biased upward because respondents opted in. Read it as a ceiling. The salary
      ladder by credential is the more useful planning table: $52,000 at certificate level, $58,000 associate, $65,000
      bachelor, $75,000 master, $85,000 doctorate. Those are first-position medians, not mid-career federal wages.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clinical dietetics was the largest first path at 28 percent. Community and public health nutrition was second at
      20 percent. Food service management was third at 14 percent, ahead of sports nutrition at 10 percent. If you
      enrolled because you wanted to work with athletes, sit with that ordering before you specialise. Sports nutrition
      is the most romanticized career in the field. The survey does not invent a job census, and we will not either.
    </p>

    <h2>Headline results</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sample size</td><td>{outcomesSurvey.sample.toLocaleString()} graduates (2020 to 2025)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Survey period</td><td>{outcomesSurvey.fieldedLabel}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Employment within six months</td><td>{outcomesSurvey.headline.employedWithinSixMonths}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Supervised practice critical or very important</td><td>{outcomesSurvey.headline.supervisedPracticeCritical}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Employers reporting difficulty hiring qualified RDNs</td><td>{outcomesSurvey.headline.employerHiringDifficulty}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wanted more business training for private practice</td><td>{outcomesSurvey.headline.wantedBusinessTraining}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Median starting salary by credential</h2>
    <p>
      These are self-reported first-position base pay figures. They are not adjusted for cost of living. A $65,000
      bachelor-level start in a high-cost metropolitan area is not the same outcome as the same number in a rural county.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Highest credential at first position</th><th>Median starting salary</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {outcomesSurvey.startingSalaryByCredential.map(([label, salary]) => (
            <tr><td>{label}</td><td>${salary.toLocaleString()}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      For federal context, dietitians and nutritionists earned a May 2024 median of ${rdn.median.toLocaleString()} across
      about {rdn.employment.toLocaleString()} jobs, against ${bls.allOccupationsMedian.toLocaleString()} for all
      occupations. That is a full-career median. Ours is an entry snapshot. Use both, and do not average them.
    </p>

    <h2>Where graduates actually went</h2>
    <p>
      Clinical dietetics is the largest destination. Food service management outranks sports nutrition. That ordering is
      the opposite of how most students talk about the field, and it is the point of publishing the table.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Career path</th><th>Share of respondents</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {outcomesSurvey.careerPaths.map(([path, share]) => (
            <tr><td>{path}</td><td>{share}%</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Sports nutrition at 10 percent is larger than the paid job market would suggest. That almost certainly reflects
      part-time, hybrid, and self-employed arrangements being counted as a path. If you are planning a sports career,
      read the <a class="text-link" href="/resources/sports-nutrition-career-guide/">sports nutrition career guide</a>
      before you specialise. There is no reliable published census of full-time sports dietitian posts, and we will not
      invent one.
    </p>

    <h2>Skills employers asked for</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Share reporting employer demand</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {outcomesSurvey.skillsInDemand.map(([skill, share]) => (
            <tr><td>{skill}</td><td>{share}%</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Counseling and communication ranks third, above every technical specialisation except assessment. Food service
      management outranks public health nutrition. Nutrition curricula do not allocate time in that order, which is why
      {outcomesSurvey.headline.wantedBusinessTraining} percent later wished they had more business training.
    </p>

    <h2>What this means for you</h2>
    <p>
      If you want clinical work, get on an ACEND accredited pathway that includes supervised practice. The survey does not
      say prestige predicts outcomes. It says practice hours and the credential do. See the
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/rankings/best-acend-accredited-nutrition-programs/">ACEND accredited ranking</a>
      and the <a class="text-link" href="/resources/how-to-become-a-registered-dietitian-step-by-step/">RDN pathway</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want sports nutrition, treat it as a specialisation on top of a clinical credential, not instead of one.
      If you want food service management, stop treating it as a consolation prize: 14 percent of respondents landed
      there, and the occupation is roughly four times the size of dietitians and nutritionists.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then read the <a class="text-link" href="/careers/">career guides</a>, pick a
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/states/">state</a>, and verify accreditation with ACEND rather than with us.
    </p>

    <h2>How to use this if you are choosing a program</h2>
    <p>
      Use the salary table to set a ceiling on debt, not a promise of income. A bachelor-level median start of $65,000
      does not service six figures of student loans in a high-cost city. Use the career-path table to test whether the
      specialisation you want is actually a first job or a later move. Use the skills table to audit a curriculum: if a
      program does not teach medical nutrition therapy, nutrition assessment, and counseling, it is not preparing you
      for the jobs that hired most of our respondents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the supervised-practice finding as a filter. If a program cannot name sites, hours, and who owns placement
      failure, it is asking you to ignore the result 94 percent of graduates agreed on. That is not a ranking nuance.
      It is the decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      Employers should read the hiring-difficulty figure next to the skills table. Difficulty finding qualified RDN
      candidates is not the same as difficulty finding people with a nutrition degree. The gap is accreditation,
      supervised practice, and medical nutrition therapy. Post accordingly.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations, in full</h2>
    <div class="research">
      <strong>Read these before citing anything above</strong>
      {outcomesSurvey.limitations.map(limitation => <p>{limitation}</p>)}
    </div>
    <p>
      The full research record, including the Program Quality Index, is on the
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/research/">research page</a>. Scoring rules are on
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/methodology/">methodology</a>. We track {stats.programs} programs at
      {' '}{stats.schools} institutions, which is not a complete census.
    </p>

    <Faq items={faq} />

    <div class="notice">
      <Byline author={author} prefix="Research directed by" />
      <strong>How to cite:</strong> {outcomesSurvey.name}, NutritionSchools.org, {outcomesSurvey.published}. Carry the
      limitations with the figure. Nothing here is medical or dietary advice. Last reviewed {site.reviewedLabel}.
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
